################ astral-sh/ty#4051
Equality narrowing for strings/ints/bytes is inconsistent with reachability analysis | OPEN  | created 2026-07-21 closed - | labels: bug,narrowing,control flow,unreachable-code | author AlexWaygood
--- body
### Summary

The first function here causes us to emit an `invalid-return-type` diagnostic: our reachability analysis determines that there are possible paths through the function that lead to the function implicitly returning `None`. The second function does not, however: the `reveal_type` call at the end of the function is inferred as returning `Never` (because the type of `x` at that point in the code is inferred as being `Never`), adding an additional constraint to the control-flow graph and leading ty to conclude that there is no path through the function that would lead to ty implicitly returning `None`: 

```py
def f(x: str) -> int:  # error: [invalid-return-type]
    if x == 'foo':
        return 42
    elif x != 'foo':
        return 42


def g(x: str) -> int:  # no error
    if x == 'foo':
        return 42
    elif x != 'foo':
        return 42

    reveal_type(x)  # revealed: Never
```

This feels pretty confusing and inconsistent. In runs of ty where `strict-literal-narrowing` is set to `false`, I think we have to try to have our reachability analysis match our type-narrowing analysis. The implicit `else` branch after the `elif` branch (the implicit fallthrough branch taken if `x != 'foo' and x == 'foo'`) should be understood as unreachable when this mode (now the default mode of ty) is enabled. That would lead us to accept both functions when `strict-literal-narrowing` is set to `false` and reject both functions when `strict-literal-narrowing` is set to `true`.

### Version

_No response_
--- comments (1)
[kimjune01 2026-07-22] Reversing the two conditions makes this go away, with no `reveal_type` involved:

```py
def eq_first(x: str) -> int:  # error: [invalid-return-type]
    if x == "foo":
        return 42
    elif x != "foo":
        return 42

def ne_first(x: str) -> int:  # no error
    if x != "foo":
        return 42
    elif x == "foo":
        return 42
```

Reachability already reads the narrowed type in both, so what differs is whether the residue makes the second comparison decidable: `Literal["foo"]` gives `Literal[True]`, while `str & ~Literal["foo"]` gives `bool`, which is the "Negative contributions" gap in `comparison/intersections.md`.
################ astral-sh/ty#4039
Membership and equality narrowing are inconsistent | CLOSED COMPLETED | created 2026-07-19 closed 2026-07-19T20:01:36Z | labels: bug,narrowing | author AlexWaygood
--- body
### Summary

Here's our current behaviour:

```py
class Foo: ...

def f(x: Foo | None, y: list[Foo], z: Foo):
    if x == z:
        reveal_type(x)  # Foo | None, reflecting the possibility that `z`
                        # could be an instance of a `Foo` subclass that compares equal to `None`
    
    if x in y:
        reveal_type(x)  # Foo -- but `list.__contains__` uses equality semantics internally,
                        # so why should this be different?
```

Here's an example of the kind of thing where this can get us into trouble:

```pycon
>>> class Foo: ...
>>> class Bar(Foo):
...     def __eq__(self, other):
...         return True
...
>>> x: list[Foo] = [Bar()]
>>> None in x
True
```

The bug is caused by the use of `is_single_valued` here: https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/blob/8bbdb8e392e13481db33abc731546eec5a827d36/crates/ty_python_semantic/src/types/narrow.rs#L3034-L3055 (`None` is a single-valued type). I don't think there's actually any reason why we should be treating single-valued types differently to other types here.

### Version

