[AlexWaygood 2026-07-19] Actually, no: while #3419 discusses the same root problem, I think "consider unsound equality narrowing of unions" and "make `==` and `in` narrowing consistent" are materially different issues. Whether or not we decide we want to narrow unions soundly in this kind of case, there's no principled reason for doing something different for `in` narrowing than there is for `==` narrowing, since `in` just calls `__eq__` under the hood in just the same way as `==`.

I think we should first make our `in` narrowing consistent by falling back to our generalised equality-inference logic instead of calling `.is_single_valued()`, and then (after doing that) consider whether we want to relax our soundness 
