################ astral-sh/ty#4750
Chain `or`/`and` short-circuiting inferring type that can't ever occur at runtime | OPEN  | created 2026-10-18 closed - | labels: control flow,runtime semantics | author svioletg
--- body
### Summary

At runtime, there is no way (that I can find) for the expression `(a or None) and a.x` to ever result in a value the type of `a`, regardless of how `__bool__` is defined for it. If `a` is truthy, the expression becomes `a and a.x`, since we already know `bool(a)` is True, we'll guaranteed get the value of `a.x`. If `a` is falsy, it becomes `None and a.x`, which will return `None`. `ty` however infers this from the expression: `(A & ~AlwaysFalsy & ~AlwaysTruthy) | None | int`

I don't know if this is something too specific for `ty` to realistically deal with, I understand if this simply isn't planned to be accounted for.

Playground link: https://play.ty.dev/12702a1e-fc7d-4932-b1c1-c5775acfc2fc

```python
from typing import reveal_type

from dataclasses import dataclass

@dataclass
class A:
    x: int | None

    def __bool__(self) -> bool:
        return (self.x is not None) and (self.x > 0)

a = A(1)
b = A(0)
c = None

reveal_type(a or None) # Correct
reveal_type(a and a.x) # Correct
# "A" is still an inferred possible type here, although at runtime this expression could only ever possibly result in "int | None"
reveal_type((a or None) and a.x)
reveal_type((b or None) and a.x)

assert ((a or None) and a.x) == 1
assert ((b or None) and b.x) is None
assert ((c or None) and c.x) is None

d = A(0)
# Object of type `(A & ~AlwaysFalsy & ~AlwaysTruthy) | None | int` is not assignable to attribute `x` of type `int | None` (invalid-assignment)
d.x = (a or None) and a.x
assert d.x == 1
# ditto
d.x = (b or None) and b.x
assert d.x is None
```

### Version

ty 0.0.89
--- comments (1)
[carljm 2026-10-19] Thanks! The runtime behavior is subtle here; in CPython 3.12 and 3.13 it is possible for `(a or None) and a.x` to evaluate to `a`, because `bool(a)` is tested twice, and it could technically be unstable (return `True` from one call and `False` from another). In 3.14 the compiler was fixed to only evaluate `bool(a)` once in this expression, making it impossible again. (See https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/124285).

I think that ideally we would model the 3.14+ behavior here, though it may not be a simple implementation.
finished
